MCP protocol transcripts
========================

Witnesses for the JSON-RPC contract in `src/skodun/mcpserver.py`, driven by the
harness in `tests/test_mcpserver.py` (which is where the format is normative).
Each `.jsonl` file is a session: the lines a client sends, and the responses the
server owes for them, in order.

File format
-----------

One directive per line. Anything else is a broken fixture, not input.

    # ...            a comment. Not sent, not counted.
    (blank line)     ignored, same as a comment.
    !<directive>     a harness flag for the whole file (see below).
    > <payload>      send `<payload>` + "\n" to the server's stdin, UTF-8.
                     `>` alone sends a bare newline; the payload keeps its own
                     leading and trailing whitespace verbatim.
    >b64 <base64>    send exactly these bytes + "\n". For lines that are not
                     valid UTF-8, carry NUL bytes, or are pure whitespace that
                     an editor would trim.
    < <json object>   the next response line must match this PREDICATE.

The harness reads the whole session, then matches the `<` predicates against the
response lines IN ORDER and asserts there are exactly as many responses as
predicates: an extra line on stdout is a failure even when every predicate
passed, because stdout carries nothing but JSON-RPC. A file with no `<` lines
asserts stdout is empty.

A predicate is matched as a SUBSET, recursively: every key it names must be
present and match, keys it does not name are free, and lists must have the same
length and match element-wise. Scalars compare equal. Four escapes exist for the
places where an exact value would be either brittle or unpinnable:

    {"$type": "object"|"array"|"string"|"number"|"boolean"|"null"}
    {"$contains": "substring"}      the value is a string containing it
    {"$absent": true}               the key must NOT be present
    {"$keys": ["a", "b"]}           the object has EXACTLY these keys
    {"$version": "ours"}            equals `mcpserver.MCP_PROTOCOL_VERSION`

File-level directives:

    !requires-fakes        the session calls the test tool/prompt registry
                           (`echo`, `refuse`, `boom`, `review`, `demo-prompt`),
                           so it runs in-process only. Files without it run
                           BOTH in-process and against a real `skodun mcp`
                           subprocess, which is what proves the shipped entry
                           point emits no stray stdout byte of its own.
    !assert no-handler-calls   at the end of the session, no tool handler was
                           invoked at all. This is how "ignored WITHOUT
                           execution" is pinned as an absence of side effects
                           rather than as an absence of output.

Provenance
----------

The fixture-provenance rule says captures beat hand-writing. Here it applies to
exactly one thing -- the handshake -- and cannot apply to the rest: no MCP
client emits a batch array, a NUL byte, or an id-less `tools/call`, so an attack
transcript has no capture to be taken from. Those files are marked SYNTHESIZED
and say, in the file, which pinned decision each line comes from. The wording
source for every synthesized file is the Task 13 protocol decision list (the
Phase 3 plan, "Task 13: MCP stdio server core"), which fixed the codes and the
shapes before any of this was written; the exception messages are this
implementation's own and are matched by `$contains`, not by equality, so a
reworded diagnostic is not a protocol change.

handshake-claude-code.jsonl
    CAPTURED (the three `>` payloads, byte-for-byte). Recorded 2026-07-30 from
    the claude-code CLI 2.1.118 acting as an MCP client (`claude -p
    --mcp-config` pointed at a stub server that logged every line it received
    and answered a minimal handshake). It asks for protocol revision
    2025-11-25. The `<` predicates are synthesized: the stub was a capture
    device, not an oracle, so its replies pin nothing.
handshake-codex-cli.jsonl
    CAPTURED the same way and on the same day, from the codex CLI 0.144.5
    (`codex exec -c mcp_servers...` against the same stub). It asks for
    2025-06-18 and puts `_meta.progressToken` in `tools/list` params. The two
    captures disagreeing about the revision is exactly why negotiation is a
    rule (echo a recognized revision, else answer with ours) and not a constant.
    The remaining lines of both captures (`notifications/initialized`,
    `tools/list`) are included as the documented context they were recorded in.
version-unrecognized.jsonl
    SYNTHESIZED. The other half of the negotiation rule, plus the missing-field
    and wrong-type cases. No client sends an unknown revision on purpose.
pre-init.jsonl
    SYNTHESIZED. `ping`/`initialize` allowed pre-handshake, everything else
    -32002 "server not initialized", and the refusal is not a latch.
garbage-bytes.jsonl
    SYNTHESIZED. Invalid UTF-8, non-JSON text, a truncated object, NUL bytes,
    and a bare number -- each answered once, id null, loop still serving.
batch-and-non-objects.jsonl
    SYNTHESIZED. Arrays rejected -32600 (MCP removed batching), non-object
    JSON, a missing/wrong `jsonrpc`, a missing/wrong `method`, and ids of types
    JSON-RPC does not allow.
unknown-method.jsonl
    SYNTHESIZED. -32601 for methods outside the served subset, silence for the
    same names sent as notifications.
malformed-params.jsonl
    SYNTHESIZED. Every -32602 shape inside a known method, and the three
    tolerated absences (`arguments` missing, `arguments: null`, `params: null`).
notifications-interleaved.jsonl
    SYNTHESIZED. Notifications between requests; string, zero, negative, empty
    and above-2^53 ids echoed exactly.
idless-request-only-methods.jsonl
    SYNTHESIZED. The id-less `initialize` / `tools/call` / `prompts/get` rule,
    with `!assert no-handler-calls` for the side-effect half.
immediate-eof.jsonl
    SYNTHESIZED, and deliberately empty: stdin closed with nothing said.
blank-lines.jsonl
    SYNTHESIZED. Bare newlines and whitespace-only lines are framing, not
    messages; indentation around a real message is tolerated.
tool-results.jsonl
    SYNTHESIZED. The `{content, isError, structuredContent}` envelope, a
    non-zero status, a raising handler, and the `tools/list` entry shape.
long-running-tool.jsonl
    SYNTHESIZED. The long-running tool's result is an ordinary tool result. Its
    concurrency (busy, read-loop liveness, EOF join) is pinned in the test
    module instead: two responses in flight have no deterministic order here.
prompts.jsonl
    SYNTHESIZED. `prompts/list` / `prompts/get` over a fake prompt; the real
    `review-now` and `gate-check` prompts arrive in Task 14.

The 8 MiB line cap has no file here on purpose: an 8-mebibyte fixture would be
committed weight for a boundary that a loop generates in a millisecond. It is
GENERATED at test runtime (`test_an_oversized_line_...`), both sides of the
boundary.
